# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
def G(a): return (a,'Each group'+a[len('Your group'):]) if a.startswith('Your group') else a
CI={1:{1:("Strategic judgement begins with the person exercising it; your learning intention anchors the unit in a challenge you lead today.","Strategic judgement begins with the person exercising it; each participant’s learning intention anchors the unit in a challenge they lead today."),
2:"While it is happening, bias often feels like experience, confidence, caution, expertise or agreement. The strategic discipline is learning to recognise what may be pulling our judgement before that judgement becomes a decision, commitment or action.",
3:"Unexamined bias turns one strategy into competing functional priorities, and its cost shows in decisions, delays and lost value.",
4:"Every phase of the strategy journey carries its own biases, and the emotional climate decides whether leaders surface them or defend them.",
5:"Collective intelligence emerges when leadership teams build practices that bring diverse perspectives into every strategic decision.",
6:("Your bias profile shows where your judgement needs support; your Problem Charter gives your group the challenge it will lead through every later unit.","Each participant’s bias profile shows where their judgement needs support; the Problem Charter gives each group the challenge it leads through every later unit.")},
2:{1:"A strategy becomes usable when its architecture sets the choices, its intent states the ambition and its Success in Practice describes the reality the enterprise must produce.",2:"Once agreed, the Strategy Intent tests every initiative for strategic fit and Success in Practice tests it for strategic contribution.",3:"A credible picture of success shows observable evidence in every domain and holds the four domains in balance.",4:"Each output builds on the one before: the architecture frames the intent, and the intent defines the success the organisation must see in practice.",5:G("Your group’s strategy now has a structure, an ambition and a picture of success that every later unit builds on.")},
3:{1:"KISS reads the organisation against its Success in Practice; OKRs turn that reading into measurable commitments.",2:"Objectives and Key Results hold when they rest on an honest assessment of what to keep, improve, start and stop.",3:"Enterprise OKRs emerge when leaders recognise their functional emphasis and balance it across the four Success in Practice domains.",4:"Strategic imagination becomes executable once each objective clears the tests of operating reality and measurable change.",5:G("Your group’s Enterprise OKRs now trace back through its KISS map to Success in Practice, giving the enterprise one measurable trajectory.")},
4:{1:"A Key Result is strategically sound when the conditions it depends on hold at each of the four checkpoints.",2:"Defining the market by customer outcomes widens the field of competition leaders see and test.",3:"Each executive brings a predictable blind spot; collective review covers the checkpoints any one function overlooks.",4:"Every failure in the exercise traces back to a checkpoint left untested; testing it early protects the direction.",5:G("Your group’s Key Results now carry named Must-Be-True conditions and executives accountable for verifying them.")},
5:{1:"Leaders who address both the mind and the heart prepare people to understand, accept and act on the change a strategy requires.",2:"Execution ignites when people move from compliance to genuine adoption of the change.",3:"The team’s behavioural profile shows where alignment is strong and where it carries risk for the strategy.",4:"A leadership team aligns people when its members interpret the strategy together and speak about the change with one voice.",5:G("Your group has stated the change and the leadership behaviours that will hold it in place.")},
6:{1:"Performance management connects enterprise results to the contributions of people and the conditions the organisation provides.",2:"Performance management bridges strategy and execution when it builds commitment to results.",3:"Executive perspectives on performance converge when performance management becomes continuous, developmental and team-based.",4:"One Key Result shows how mapped contributions and FACES together make people and organisational performance manageable.",5:G("Your group’s Enterprise Performance Management System now links one Key Result to its contributions, their management and its vital check-up.")},
7:{1:"An expectation becomes owned when it is clear, realistic and accepted by the person who delivers it, with the conditions the organisation provides.",2:"Expectation alignment drives ownership; where it fails, engagement and accountability decline first.",3:"Each executive function carries a characteristic expectation gap, and surface agreement can conceal it.",4:"The Alignment Dialogue secures what each person will deliver and what the organisation will enable.",5:G("Your group’s delivery expectations now carry named owners, enabling conditions and an agreed dialogue.")},
8:{1:"Performance measurement makes execution visible, and the Review–Realign–Restore cycle turns what it shows into recovery.",2:"Review discipline sustains execution momentum; when it weakens, warning signals, accountability, learning and credibility weaken with it.",3:"Measurement breaks down at predictable fractures, and each executive function contributes its own performance gap.",4:"An executive team turns individual performance signals into enterprise intelligence when it reviews them together.",5:G("Your group’s decision points now set when and how the team acts on each consequence of weak review discipline.")},
9:{1:"A strategic clog sits where people or organisational conditions block the flow from intent to execution, and the Three Performance Lines of Sight locate it.",2:"Clogs clear when leaders detect, classify and resolve them with the lever that matches the clog.",3:"Executive decisions shape the conditions in which people and teams perform. The Three Performance Lines of Sight help leaders recognise how decisions within their own functions may influence results elsewhere in the organisation.",4:"Unclogging becomes a habit when the leadership team owns friction collectively and works through it in a regular cycle.",5:G("Your group has traced a real challenge to its clog, chosen the lever and set a Review–Realign–Restore response with clear ownership.")},
10:{1:"Each strategic commitment depends on specific organisational capabilities, and ESRG names them domain by domain.",2:"A functional ESRG profile shows where operating conditions enable a strategic commitment and where they constrain it.",3:"Executive decisions shape organisational operating conditions through the priorities, assumptions and practices applied within each function. Effective ESRG alignment requires leaders to recognise how their decisions influence operating capabilities beyond their portfolios and challenge assumptions that undermine strategic delivery.",4:"Collective ESRG intelligence develops as executives coordinate what functions have in common, reconcile what they experience differently and align the operating dependencies required for enterprise results.",5:"Functional assessments make operating conditions visible. Collective intelligence establishes what those conditions mean for the enterprise. The Collective Intelligence Response determines what must change; the ESRG Alignment Intervention Plan establishes how the executive team will make that change happen."},
11:{5:"Organisational health improves when the executive team reads it from evidence, prices what poor health costs, acts on the lowest-scoring conditions with named accountability, and models the norms of its Cultural Operating Code."}}
TR={1:{1:"Section 2 examines what behavioural strategy is: how intuitive thinking, functional expertise and cognitive bias shape strategic judgement.",2:"Section 3 examines why behavioural strategy is critical, through a case of functional bias and the hidden cost of poor behavioural awareness.",3:"Section 4 examines where bias and emotional climate operate across the phases of the strategy journey.",4:"Section 5 examines how leadership teams turn individual thinking into collective intelligence.",5:("You assess your own bias profile, and your group sets out the challenge it will address in its Problem Charter.","Participants assess their own bias profile, and each group sets out the challenge it will address in its Problem Charter.")},
2:{1:"Section 2 examines why the Strategy Intent and Success in Practice matter once defined, and how they shape alignment, choices and execution.",2:"Section 3 examines Success in Practice in depth: the observable indicators in each domain and the distortions that unbalance the picture of success.",3:"Section 4 follows one company through the three connected outputs, from Strategy Architecture to Strategy Intent to Success in Practice.",4:G("Your group builds the same three outputs for its simulated company and agrees them in the group record.")},
3:{1:"Section 2 examines why the KISS-to-OKR sequence determines whether OKRs hold up in execution.",2:"Section 3 examines where each leadership role’s natural emphasis concentrates OKRs, and how those hot zones distort enterprise priorities.",3:"Section 4 puts the sequence into practice through Strategy Airport, taking one Success in Practice statement through to an execution-ready OKR.",4:G("Your group translates its own Success in Practice into a KISS map and then into Enterprise OKRs.")},
4:{1:"Section 2 examines why industry definitions mislead strategy, and how customer outcomes reveal the full field of competition.",2:"Section 3 examines where directional integrity breaks down: the checkpoint each executive role is most likely to overlook.",3:"Section 4 applies the checkpoints to realistic strategic failures, matching each breakdown to its cause.",4:G("Your group stress-tests its own Key Results on the four checkpoints and assigns ownership for verifying each condition.")},
5:{1:"Section 2 examines why aligning heart and mind determines execution outcomes, and why change must be led to adoption.",2:"Section 3 examines where the leadership team stands, through the behavioural profile each member and the team bring to the strategy.",3:"Section 4 examines how the leadership team converges its perspectives into one interpretation and one message.",4:G("Your group states the change its strategy requires and the leadership behaviours that will hold it in place.")},
6:{1:"Section 2 examines why performance management is the bridge between strategy and execution.",2:"Section 3 examines where performance management becomes vulnerable across executive perspectives, and how its practice is evolving.",3:"Section 4 brings the unit together in one worked example: the contributions a Key Result requires and how FACES manages them.",4:G("Your group builds its Contribution Map, applies FACES to it and sets the WARD check-up for its Key Result.")},
7:{1:"Section 2 examines why establishing expectations determines execution outcomes.",2:"Section 3 examines where the expectations gap emerges across executive functions.",3:"Section 4 practises the Alignment Dialogue across leadership roles.",4:G("Your group establishes delivery expectations for two contributions from its Contribution Map and prepares the Alignment Dialogue with the board chair.")},
8:{1:"Section 2 examines why review discipline determines execution velocity.",2:"Section 3 examines where performance measurement breaks down across functions.",3:"Section 4 builds the collective mechanism through which the executive team reviews performance together.",4:G("Your group sets decision points against the four consequences of weak review discipline and tests its WARD check-up.")},
9:{1:"Section 2 examines why disciplined intervention clears strategic clogs, and the levers that clear them.",2:"Section 3 examines where executive decisions and practices contribute to performance clogs.",3:"Section 4 examines how executive leaders work collectively to remove performance clogs and restore strategic coherence and performance momentum.",4:G("Your group returns to an original work-based challenge, identifies its clog and applies the Unclogging Cycle.")},
10:{1:"Section 2 examines why each ESRG domain enables execution, and how a functional profile shows the condition of each.",2:"Section 3 examines where executive biases distort the operating capabilities ESRG describes.",3:"Section 4 examines how executive leaders reconcile these perspectives and establish organisational adjustments to strengthen enterprise-wide execution capability.",4:"The capstone executive team now has the functional evidence needed to populate its Enterprise ESRG Scanner and determine targeted alignment interventions."},
11:{1:"Section 2 examines why organisational health determines execution performance: how it multiplies execution capability, what poor health costs the enterprise and how it sustains performance over time."}}
